THE CASE AGAINST THE TARGET:
SRC_001 proposes computing skeptical intersection of all admissible fixed points via alternating graph reachability, claiming polynomial complexity because 'admissible sets form an antimatroid and membership can be decided by two reachability queries.' This is a mischaracterization: the family of admissible sets in abstract argumentation is generally not an antimatroid (the accessible union property fails for arbitrary attack graphs), and the decision problem 'is a node in every maximal admissible set' is equivalent to grounded extension membership only under the standard grounded semantics, not under SRC_003's redefined acceptance as intersection over *all* admissible fixed points. If the target truly means all admissible sets (not just maximal ones), the intersection is the *ideal* semantics, whose membership problem is not known to be polynomial. Thus the stated complexity guarantee relies on a false structural claim, violating the polynomial-cost preservation requirement.

TARGET:
Abandon the single fixed point in favor of a set of admissible fixed points and define acceptance as membership in all of them (skeptical intersection), but compute it with alternating graph reachability: a node is accepted iff every maximal admissible set contains it, which is equivalent to the node being in the grounded extension by a known duality. This strengthens the semantics by making it robust to arbitrary changes in the attack graph and gives a natural proof of reinstatement as a set-inclusion property; complexity remains polynomial because admissible sets form an antimatroid and membership can be decided by two reachability queries. Criticism: the equivalence may rely on assumptions about finite graphs that do not hold for dynamic warrant generation.